different keys will inevitably map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps resolve these conflicts and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### Separate Addressing (Chaining)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value at each array index, each \"bucket\" in the array points to a **linked list** (or another data structure). \n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Lookup:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index, then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Optimization:** In modern implementations (like Java 8+), if a linked list grows too long, it is converted into a **Balanced Binary Search Tree**, which keeps lookups fast.\n\n#### Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements stay within the primary array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the next available empty slot.\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** Uses a quadratic formula (e.g., $i+1^2, i+2^2$) to avoid"}]}
